{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.847,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "Each circled number is the product of the numbers that are to its left and below. For example, $30 = 2 \\times 15, \\quad 15 = 3 \\times 5$ . Each time, a composite number is factorised so that, at least one factor is prime. We stop when we reach a prime number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.166,
            0.846,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "Can you write the prime factorisation of 105 and 30 using these two figures?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.203,
            0.712,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "We collect the prime factors along the left and the one at the bottom. We then construct the prime factorisation as shown in the figure."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.26,
            0.712,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "While carrying out factorisation, the circles are usually left out and the steps are carried out in this format:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.308,
            0.382,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc}3 & 105 &  \\\\\\hline5 & 35 &  \\\\\\hline& 7 &  \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.48,
            0.308,
            0.579,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc}& 30 &  &  \\\\\\hline3 & \\boxed{15} &  &  \\\\\\hline& 5 &  &  \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.196,
            0.872,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.742,
            0.375,
            0.882,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "105 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 7",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.39,
            0.588,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "Let us call this method the **division method**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.409,
            0.847,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "Try finding the prime factorisation of 1200 using the method above. If we had used the earlier method, our calculation would have been as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.472,
            0.655,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "1200 = 40 \\times 30 = 5 \\times 8 \\times 5 \\times 6 = \\ldots",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.5,
            0.547,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "Which calculation is easier to carry out?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.538,
            0.719,
            0.559
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Factors of a Number Using Prime Factorisation"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.564,
            0.847,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "From the prime factorisation of a number, we can construct all its factors. This can be used to simplify the procedure for finding the HCF of two numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.622,
            0.844,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "Consider the number 840 and its prime factorisation $2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.65,
            0.457,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "Is $2 \\times 2 \\times 7 = 28$ a factor of 840?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.693,
            0.629,
            0.716
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "If yes, what should it be multiplied by to get 840?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.719,
            0.846,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "To answer these questions, we can reorder the prime factors of 840 as follows (recall that reordering factors does not change the product):"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.768,
            0.635,
            0.787
          ],
          "content": "8 4 0 = ( 2 \\times 2 \\times 7 ) \\times ( 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 ) .",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.795,
            0.188,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.431,
            0.812,
            0.566,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": "8 4 0 = 2 8 \\times 3 0 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.84,
            0.846,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $( 2 \\times 2 \\times 7 ) = 2 8$ is a factor of 840, and it should be multiplied by 30 $( 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 )$ to get 840."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.949
          ],
          "content": "50"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
